{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And they came to Elim, where there were twelve springs of water and seventy date-palms.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the reason Scripture elaborated to tell this is to teach you that this place was the opposite of the first, for in the first place the waters were evil and bitter, whereas here they were beautiful and sweet, since date-palms thrive and succeed only in a place whose waters are sweet. Therefore they lingered in this place — that is what it says, ״ויחנו שם על המים״ — for nature craves flowing cold water in the summer season, in the manner of what the prophet Jeremiah, peace be upon him, said (Jeremiah 18:14): ״היעזוב מצור שדי שלג לבנון אם ינתשו מים זרים קרים נוזלים״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ״twelve springs of water and seventy date-palms״ — Rabbi Eliezer ha-Moda'i says: From the day the Holy One, blessed be He, created His world, He created there twelve springs corresponding to the twelve tribes of Israel, and seventy date-palms corresponding to the seventy elders. The explanation of this Midrash is that from the time Israel departed from the Sea of Reeds they did not come to a good place until they arrived here at Elim. And just as a miracle was performed for them at the sea in the sweetening of its waters and in twelve channels for the twelve tribes, so it was here at Elim: its waters were sweet, living flowing water, and twelve springs for the twelve tribes, and likewise seventy date-palms for the seventy elders, in order to bestow honor upon each and every tribe, and likewise upon each and every one of the elders." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״And they came to Elim, where there were twelve springs of water and seventy date-palms״ — now that they came to this place, Elim, they entered into that apprehension called אלהים, and this is the language of ״ויבאו אילמה ושם שתים עשרה עינות מים״, for they apprehended there twelve angels, three for each direction of the four directions of the world, and each three of them with their hosts, a great camp, and they are the ones called the four camps of the Shekhinah. And ״seventy date-palms״ — they are the seventy angels that surround the Throne of Glory and are appointed over the seventy nations of the world, each one appointed over its nation. And so you find in Sefer ha-Bahir: ״And what is ושם י״ב עינות מים? The Holy One, blessed be He, gave them springs of water at first; afterward He restored to them stones, as it is written (Joshua 4:9) ושתים עשרה אבנים. What is the reason? Because at first the Torah in the world was compared to water, and afterward it was fixed in a fixed place — which water does not do, being here today and here tomorrow. And the matter of seventy date-palms teaches that the Holy One, blessed be He, has seventy קומות (levels), and they draw from twelve פשוטות (simple ones) — just as these waters are פשוטים, so they too are פשוטים. And whence do we know that תמר is קומה? As it is written (Song of Songs 7:8) זאת קומתך דמתה לתמר. And not only that, but in date-palms there are seventy species, as it is written ושבעים תמרים, and no two were alike, and the deeds of this one do not resemble those of that one, and the character (טעם) of this one does not resemble that of that one — end of quotation. And since each and every one acts according to its appointment as it was appointed, and is not authorized to enter the boundary of its fellow, therefore it says they are not alike, and the deed of this one does not resemble the deed of that one. And this is hinted to you: from these twelve springs, which are the four camps of the Shekhinah, there draw the seventy date-palms, which are the angels surrounding the Throne of Glory — it compared them to date-palms on account of קומה. And behold, their likeness came forth from Jacob: twelve tribes divided into four camps, and from them were seventy souls. And understand this little bit and you will find the greatness of Jacob, who was called אל, as it is written (Genesis 33:20) ״ויקרא לו אל אלהי ישראל״, which our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: who called him אל אלהי ישראל? And you shall know and comprehend that all the matters of the patriarchs and the commandments of the Torah are all forms and likenesses to attain and to know the knowledge of the holy ones."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:03.907121192Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}